{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "What is workforce management in contact centers?",
"acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Workforce Management (WFM) in contact centers is the systematic process of forecasting inbound contact volumes, scheduling the right number of agents to meet service level targets, and monitoring real-time performance to make intraday adjustments. WFM encompasses demand forecasting, capacity planning, schedule optimization, and intraday management." }
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "How do you calculate staffing for a call center?",
"acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Call center staffing is calculated using the Erlang C formula, which accounts for call arrival rate, average handle time (AHT), and target service level. The basic steps are: (1) Forecast call volume per interval, (2) Calculate workload = calls × AHT, (3) Apply Erlang C to determine raw agents needed, (4) Apply shrinkage to get scheduled headcount. KBM simplifies this by calculating effective agents from scheduled headcount minus shrinkage." }
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "What is the Erlang C staffing model?",
"acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Erlang C is a mathematical formula developed by A.K. Erlang used to calculate the probability of calls being queued and the number of agents required to meet a given service level. It considers traffic intensity (calls per interval × AHT ÷ interval seconds), number of agents, and service level target. Most WFM software uses Erlang C as the underlying staffing engine." }
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "How do WFM teams forecast call volumes?",
"acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "WFM teams forecast call volumes using historical data analysis, trend identification, and seasonality modeling. Common methods include weighted moving averages, exponential smoothing, and regression analysis. The forecast is typically produced at interval level (15 or 30 minutes) and validated using forecast bias metrics: Bias% = (Actual − Forecast) / Forecast × 100." }
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "What is arrival variability in call centers?",
"acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Arrival variability measures how unpredictable inbound contact volume is from day to day. It is calculated using the Coefficient of Variation (CV = Standard Deviation / Mean × 100). A CV below 10% indicates stable, predictable demand. CV of 10–20% is moderate variability. Above 20% means high variability, where WFM planners must build larger staffing buffers to absorb demand uncertainty." }
}
]
}
]
}
